 "extra_brief": "### This is a second pass. Read your existing shard first\n\nYour brief opens with a \"Second pass\" section. It lists the problems the checks found in your first shard, the fields you could not fill and the tasks with no sign they were opened. Do those. Nothing else is new.\n\n1. Before any research, run `cat` on your shard_path. A shard from the first pass is already there.\n2. Keep everything in it. Keep every finding, qa item, monitor item, newunits item, entity and source_log entry. Change an item only to fix a listed problem, or when new evidence makes it better. Then say so in its note.\n3. When you fill a field that was in not_found, take it out of not_found. When you still cannot fill it, leave it there and add a source_log entry that says what you searched.\n4. Write the merged shard back to the same shard_path, in the same shape. Then check that it parses with python. Your verifier calls add to the existing urls.jsonl file. Never delete that file.\n5. Budget for this pass: about 25 fetches per plant. This replaces the budget of 12 above.\n\n### Fixes the checks ask for\n\n- A dead link: run `python scripts/fetch.py <url> --head 3000`, then look up the exact address in the Wayback Machine. If a saved copy shows the value, add a source_log entry with the web.archive.org address as verified. Say in the note that it is a saved copy.\n- A page that needs a form, a script or paging, such as an Oil and Gas Watch facility page or the PJM cycle request page. Read the page source and find the address the page calls, often a JSON endpoint, or the links to each record. Or use the real-Chrome load in fetch.py. A note that blames a form, a script or paging fails the check.\n- Never write that something was not checked, \"noticed in passing\", \"not a task\" or \"I did not verify\". Research it now. Or say what you searched and what you did not find.\n- Each qa item has four parts. The question is one sentence ending in a question mark. The finding is one to three sentences. The recommendation is one or two sentences and gives an instruction. Everything else goes in researcher_notes.\n- Never use these words in any note: green, yellow, red, stage, staged, lane, tier, shard. Say \"a second source would confirm it\", not \"green\".\n- Set independent to true only when two verified links from different publishers state the value.\n- Backup Power: a Title V air permit or its statement of basis lists emergency generators and fire pump engines. The Kentucky Division for Air Quality permit is usually the best source. Louisville Metro Air Pollution Control District issues permits for Jefferson County plants.\n- Every validation report task still needs an answer, as the first-pass rules say.\n",
